Memory model
-------------
Adjacency Matrix
- 2D array representation of a graph
- For a graph with n nodes, create an n x n matrix
- If there is an edge from node i to node j, set matrix[i][j]
- If the graph is undirected, set matrix[j][i] as well
- Space complexity: O(n^2)
- Time complexity for checking if an edge exists: O(1)
Adjacency List
- Array of lists representation of a graph
- For a graph with n nodes, create an array of size n
- Each index of the array represents a node, and the list at that index contains the neighbors
- Space complexity: O(n + e), where e is the number of edges
- Time complexity for checking if an edge exists: O(k), where k is the number of neighbors of the node

Traversal algorithms
- Depth-First Search (DFS)
  - Explores as far as possible along each branch before backtracking
  - Can be implemented using recursion or a stack
  - Time complexity: O(n + e), where n is the number of nodes and e is the number of edges
  - Space complexity: O(n) for the recursion stack or O(n) for the explicit stack
- Breadth-First Search (BFS)
  - Explores all neighbors at the present depth prior to moving on to nodes at the next depth level
  - Can be implemented using a queue(FIFO)
  - Time complexity: O(n + e)
  - Space complexity: O(n) for the queue

Algorithms list for Graphs
1.Topological Sort
   - Orders the nodes in a directed acyclic graph (DAG) such that for every directed edge u -> v, node u comes before v in the ordering
   - Can be implemented using DFS or Kahn's algorithm (BFS)
   - Time complexity: O(n + e)
   - Space complexity: O(n)

2. Khan's Algorithm
   - Finds the topological ordering of a directed acyclic graph (DAG)
   - Uses a queue to process nodes with no incoming edges
   - Time complexity: O(n + e)
   - Space complexity: O(n)

3. Dijkstra's Algorithm
   - Finds the shortest path from a source node to all other nodes in a weighted graph
   - Uses a priority queue to explore the closest unvisited node
   - Time complexity: O((n + e) log n) with a binary heap
   - Space complexity: O(n )

4. Bellman-Ford Algorithm
   - Finds the shortest path from a source node to all other nodes in a weighted graph,
   - Can handle negative weight edges
   - Time complexity: O(n * e)
   - Space complexity: O(n)

5. Floyd-Warshall Algorithm
   - Finds shortest paths between all pairs of nodes in a weighted graph
   - Time complexity: O(n^3)

6. Prim's Algorithm
   - Finds the minimum spanning tree (MST) of a weighted undirected graph
   - Uses a priority queue to select the edge with the smallest weight that connects a visited node to an unvisited node
   - Time complexity: O((n + e) log n) with a binary heap
   - Space complexity: O(n)

7. Kruskal's Algorithm
   - Finds the minimum spanning tree (MST) of a weighted undirected graph
   - Uses a disjoint-set (union-find) data structure to detect cycles
   - Time complexity: O((n + e) log n) with a binary heap
   - Space complexity: O(n)


